body:
Had a component that subscribed to a websocket every time the user changed tabs. Looked fine in development because I wasn't sitting there for long.

Then I noticed the same event was firing four times after switching tabs a few times.

Turns out I was creating the subscription inside an effect and never cleaning it up.

```js
useEffect(() => {
  const socket = connectToRoom(roomId);
  socket.on("message", handleMessage);
  return () => socket.close();
}, [roomId]);
```

 The annoying part is that React wasn't doing anything weird. I just forgot that effects can represent a resource with a lifetime, not just a place to run some code.
